        <mxCell id="140" value="4 - KEY TRADE-OFFS (each vs its NAMED alternative)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=4;fontSize=15;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#1F4E79;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3720" width="800" height="30" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="141" value="Store CHANGES, not SAMPLES&#10;   vs. 5-min samples (his choice): 550 TB -&gt; ~500 GB, and a 2-yr chart goes 220k rows -&gt; ~200.&#10;   Give up: cannot distinguish &quot;price unchanged&quot; from &quot;poller was down&quot; - fixed by a daily heartbeat row.&#10;   KEY INSIGHT: polling frequency and storage frequency are INDEPENDENT decisions. He collapsed them into one.&#10;&#10;Cassandra / ClickHouse  vs.  sharded SQL (his choice)&#10;   Append-only, never updated, always range-scanned by (productId, time). That is a time-series shape.&#10;   Give up: joins, cross-product transactions, strong consistency - all three already conceded by his own NFR3.&#10;&#10;Pre-computed daily rollups  vs.  raw scan + cursor pagination (his choice)&#10;   Pagination makes it WORSE: dozens of sequential round trips inside a 1 s p99 budget.&#10;   A chart is ~1000 px wide. Give up: exact intra-day detail on long windows - nobody can see it anyway.&#10;&#10;Lease-based work queue (nextPollAt ZSET)  vs.  static cron-instance assignment (his choice)&#10;   A dead instance holding 3M assignments stales them silently - which is exactly what breaks his own&#10;   99.99% DETECTION-availability NFR. Leases expire and get re-taken. Also buys adaptive polling free:&#10;   volatile products 5 min, dormant products daily -&gt; 167k fetch/s drops ~10x at no freshness cost.&#10;&#10;Index (productId, targetPrice) + range query  vs.  scan all subscriptions for the product (his choice)&#10;   A $500 -&gt; $499 drop wakes the 20 people who wanted $499, not the 1M who wanted $300.&#10;   Same &quot;shrink the candidate set instead of filtering a large one&quot; move as his DSA round the same morning.&#10;&#10;CDN in front of chart reads  vs.  read replicas only (his choice)&#10;   Chart responses are immutable per (productId, window, resolution) until the next change - near-100% hit&#10;   rate on exactly the viral products that cause the hot key. Read replicas alone still pay full query cost." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;fillColor=#FFF2CC;strokeColor=#D6B656;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3760" width="1500" height="540" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="150" value="5 - DEEP DIVES" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=4;fontSize=15;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#1F4E79;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1600" y="3720" width="600" height="30" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="151" value="A. THE CORE HARD PROBLEM - repeat notifications  (nobody raised this all round)&#10;   Poll every 5 min. Price drops below target and STAYS there.&#10;   The consumer fires a notification every 5 minutes. Forever.&#10;   This is the most user-visible bug the system can have, and the design ships it.&#10;   Fix: `armed` flag on the subscription, cleared on fire, re-armed only when the&#10;   price rises back above target. Plus dedupe key (subscriptionId, changeId) so&#10;   at-least-once Kafka delivery does not double-fire.&#10;   He raised idempotency OF THE CONSUMER. This is idempotency OF THE USER-VISIBLE&#10;   ACTION - harder, and the one that matters.&#10;&#10;B. THE EXACT SCALE BREAK - and where it dissolves&#10;   His numbers, correct: 220k points x 50B = 11 MB per full-window chart.&#10;   200 chart loads/s x 11 MB = 2.2 GB/s egress, with a p99 &lt; 1s promise on top.&#10;   His answer: cursor pagination. That makes it worse.&#10;   Real fix: change-points + rollups. 2-yr window -&gt; ~730 rows -&gt; ~30 KB -&gt; ~6 MB/s.&#10;   The break does not get MITIGATED, it stops existing.&#10;&#10;C. READ:WRITE - the inversion he never caught&#10;   He concluded 20:1 (requests). Measured in ROWS TOUCHED: 200/s x 220k = 4.4e7&#10;   rows/s read vs 1.67e5 writes/s = ~250:1. He designed for a write-heavy system.&#10;   It is one of the most read-heavy systems he has been given.&#10;&#10;D. HOT KEYS - two of them, he found one&#10;   Read side (found, after a probe): viral product chart reads -&gt; read replicas. OK,&#10;   but CDN is the real answer since the payload is immutable between changes.&#10;   Notify side (missed): 1M subscriptions scanned on every price point for a viral&#10;   product -&gt; (productId, targetPrice) range query.&#10;&#10;E. CONSISTENCY&#10;   NFR3 already grants eventual consistency on price points - spend it. The only&#10;   ordering that matters is per-product, which Kafka partitioning by productId gives.&#10;   Notification correctness needs read-your-writes on the `armed` flag ONLY, so keep&#10;   subscriptions in a strongly-consistent store and price history in an eventual one.&#10;&#10;F. OPERABILITY - none of this came up&#10;   The metric that matters is not QPS, it is STALENESS: max age of last successful&#10;   poll, per product and per retailer domain. A retailer changes their markup, the&#10;   parser silently returns nothing, prices freeze, and every dashboard stays green.&#10;   Also: consumer lag alert tied to the 5-min NFR; DLQ for unparseable pages with a&#10;   per-domain failure-rate alert; parser-version canary; and COST - 167k egress HTTP&#10;   requests/s is a real line item, and the strongest argument for adaptive polling.&#10;&#10;G. BLACK FRIDAY (20x change rate)&#10;   Polling rate is FIXED, so ingest QPS does not move - only the change-event rate&#10;   through Kafka does, 20x. That is the backpressure question: partitions and the&#10;   consumer fleet must be sized for peak, or lag blows the 5-min freshness NFR&#10;   precisely when the notifications matter most.&#10;   His push/webhook answer is the right long-term shape." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;fillColor=#F8CECC;strokeColor=#B85450;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1600" y="3760" width="1100" height="920" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
      </root>
        </mxGraphModel>
    </diagram>
</mxfile>